  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nAfter `streamHTMLUnsafe({ runScripts: true })` on an element in an iframe's document, the element can be moved to another document and streaming continues there. But when a streamed parser-blocking script is pending, the parser resumes from a task tied to the iframe's document:\n\n> Queue a global task on the DOM manipulation task source given the parser's script target document's relevant global object to perform the following steps:\n>\n> 1. Execute the pending parsing-blocking scripts for this parser.\n> 2. If this parser's aborted is false, then run a fragment parser given this parser.\n\nThe script target document is set once, to the context document, when the stream is created. A task whose document isn't fully active isn't runnable, so if the iframe is removed before that task runs, the parser never resumes, and nothing aborts it. As far as I can tell, the rest of the content is never inserted and `close()` never settles:\n\n```js\n// div is in iframe.contentDocument\nconst writer = div.streamHTMLUnsafe({ runScripts: true }).getWriter();\nawait writer.write('<script src=\"slow.js\"></script><p>after</p>');\ndocument.body.append(div);\niframe.remove();\nawait writer.close(); // never settles\n```\n\nMaybe the task could use the target's current node document's relevant global object, or the parser could be aborted, rejecting `close()`, when the script target document stops being fully active.\n\n(Found while comparing #12758 with a rewrite of it, zcorpan/html#1.)\n",
